  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nShortwave trof overhead, contributing to the high clouds now \npresent, will drift southward to the southern lakes and OH Valley. \nCloud cover will continue to erode nw to se thru the afternoon and \nevening hours. Pinched upper ridge will then remain overhead \nthis weekend and early next week, as slow-moving nor'easter \ncontributes to a stalled upper pattern. \n\nThe relatively slow clearing today, with delayed diurnal heating/ \nmixing, should leave us more prone to fog tonight. Winds will be \neffectively calm tonight (outside of lake breezes, we have little \nwind this afternoon). Also not impossible for some spotty mid 30s \nfor lows tonight, and corresponding patchy frost. \n\nBetter mixing will be seen on Saturday, and there is drier air \navailable aloft to mix down. For fire wx users, have lowered \nguidance dew points/RHs, which reach as low as 30 percent Sat \nafternoon between CAD and MBL. Winds remain light/variable, so not\noverly concerned. \n\nOur next active wx looks unlikely until midweek, Tue night and \nbeyond. The nor'easter low opens up and ejects, and the train \nbehind it can start moving. There will also be an infusion of \neastern Pacific tropical moisture into the sw states, though for\nnow the northern and southern streams will only marginally \ninteract, at least thru midweek. Monday and Tuesday are looking \nlike the warmest days of this dry spell.",
